WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:09.390
<v Speaker 1>So if you asked a billion believers what sits at the absolute center of their faith, I mean, almost all of them would point to two things without hesitation.

2
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:16.870
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, they'd point to the historical messenger who arrived with the divine message and the revelation or scripture that messenger carried.

3
00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:17.470
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

4
00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:21.230
<v Speaker 1>That instinct is just so deeply ingrained in our collective consciousness.

5
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:27.390
<v Speaker 1>We assume the bringer of the book and the book itself must be the undisputed starting line of reality.

6
00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:27.870
<v Speaker 2>We do.

7
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.230
<v Speaker 2>It's natural to think that way.

8
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:29.830
<v Speaker 2>But

9
00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.590
<v Speaker 2>What if that hierarchy is completely upside down?

10
00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:41.710
<v Speaker 1>And that is exactly what we are exploring today for you, the listener, as we unpack A profound historical treatise called Establishing the Imitai.

11
00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.910
<v Speaker 2>It was written by the brilliant scholar, Anis Aburi.

12
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:50.350
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, and just to keep things smooth as we navigate his pretty intricate philosophical architecture.

13
00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.150
<v Speaker 1>We will simply refer to him as the author from here on out.

14
00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:57.470
<v Speaker 2>This text really demands that you rethink the very geometry of belief.

15
00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:07.630
<v Speaker 2>I mean, it argues with incredible force that the I am a mate, the continuous presence of the living guide, is not some secondary supplementary role that comes after the message.

16
00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:08.830
<v Speaker 1>It flips the whole script.

17
00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:09.070
<v Speaker 2>Yeah.

18
00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:18.030
<v Speaker 1>The author presents it as the supreme office of existence, like the absolute center of the religion, outranking even the historical messenger.

19
00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.550
<v Speaker 2>The imit is the pole and foundation of religion.

20
00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.990
<v Speaker 2>that never lapses while the messenger comes and goes.

21
00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.430
<v Speaker 1>That is just a massive paradigm shifting statement.

22
00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:26.270
<v Speaker 1>It really is.

23
00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:29.630
<v Speaker 2>And we are going to unpack that radical priority from the ground up.

24
00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:30.470
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, we are going.

25
00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:35.870
<v Speaker 1>To explore how the author argues that human nature itself proves this structure must exist.

26
00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:44.270
<v Speaker 1>And then, perhaps most fascinating of all, we will witness how he calls upon the physical universe to testify to this.

27
00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.150
<v Speaker 2>The cosmos above us and the earth below us.

28
00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:51.870
<v Speaker 2>He uses them to act as an unforgeable witness to this reality.

29
00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.150
<v Speaker 1>But he doesn't start with vague theological concepts, does he?

30
00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:56.350
<v Speaker 2>No, not at all.

31
00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:03.390
<v Speaker 2>He carves 2 very specific, highly physical images into the mind of the reader to describe this office.

32
00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.390
<v Speaker 2>He uses the language of physics and architecture to anchor us.

33
00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.070
<v Speaker 1>Okay, so what's the first image?

34
00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.990
<v Speaker 2>The first term he introduces is KOTB, the pole.

35
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:18.110
<v Speaker 2>He wants us to visualize the fixed pivot that a heavy millstone turns on.

36
00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:18.590
<v Speaker 1>Oh, I see.

37
00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:24.350
<v Speaker 1>So if you picture a traditional millstone, you have this massive crushing weight of rock grinding grain.

38
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.230
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

39
00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.230
<v Speaker 2>The kinetic energy involved is immense.

40
00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:31.950
<v Speaker 2>The millstone revolves continuously doing the heavy necessary work of the world.

41
00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:39.230
<v Speaker 1>But it can only do that work safely and effectively because there is a single perfectly still point in the very center, which is the pivot.

42
00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:43.950
<v Speaker 2>Because if you remove that central pivot, all that kinetic energy instantly becomes destructive.

43
00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:48.550
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, the stone just flies off its axis and the entire structure tears itself apart.

44
00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:55.150
<v Speaker 2>The stillness of the pole is what allows the violent movement of the stone to have purpose and direction rather than just chaotic ruin.

45
00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.430
<v Speaker 2>And that physical reality mirrors the

46
00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:58.270
<v Speaker 2>religious reality.

47
00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:07.710
<v Speaker 1>So the spinning millstone of worldly and religious affairs would just disintegrate into chaos without a fixed living center.

48
00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:09.390
<v Speaker 2>That is exactly his point.

49
00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:13.390
<v Speaker 2>Then the second term he uses moves from mechanics to architecture.

50
00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:17.390
<v Speaker 2>the foundation of religion.

51
00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:18.430
<v Speaker 1>The foundation.

52
00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:25.070
<v Speaker 1>So you have to think of the buried courses of stone upon which an entire fortress is constructed.

53
00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:28.030
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, the stones you never actually see when you look at the building.

54
00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:32.590
<v Speaker 2>You admire the soaring walls, the intricate windows, the vaulted roof.

55
00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.670
<v Speaker 1>You don't look at the dirt and the buried foundation.

56
00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:41.390
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, without those hidden stones bearing the immense weight deep in the earth, nothing above them can stand for a single moment.

57
00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:44.030
<v Speaker 1>So the author is asserting that the living imam

58
00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:48.350
<v Speaker 1>is both the axis everything revolves around and the buried foundation everything rests upon.

59
00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:52.750
<v Speaker 2>Yes, around him the affairs of God's servants are governed and the land is set right.

60
00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:58.470
<v Speaker 2>And the author claims that without this foundation, three specific realities are completely lost to the believer.

61
00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:03.390
<v Speaker 1>Let's walk through those 3 realities because this is where the stakes of this treatise become incredibly high.

62
00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.750
<v Speaker 2>Well, none of these things arrive raw into the hands of a believer.

63
00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.550
<v Speaker 2>They all flow through this single structural channel.

64
00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:15.390
<v Speaker 2>The first of these three realities is tawhid, the absolute unity of God.

65
00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.670
<v Speaker 1>The highest, most abstract truth a human mind can reach for.

66
00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:19.310
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

67
00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:26.830
<v Speaker 2>It is the absolute summit of religious knowledge, and yet it is incredibly easy for the human intellect to distort it if left unguided.

68
00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:28.030
<v Speaker 1>And the second reality?

69
00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:36.270
<v Speaker 2>The second reality is the historical message itself, the factual, grounded reality that God has spoken and sent a revelation into human history.

70
00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:37.150
<v Speaker 1>Okay, and the third?

71
00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.590
<v Speaker 2>The third reality is Tawil, the

72
00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:41.950
<v Speaker 2>interpretation of the sacred law.

73
00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:48.030
<v Speaker 1>Let's pause on that third one, the inner interpretation, because I feel like it highlights the mechanics of this dependency really well.

74
00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:48.710
<v Speaker 2>It does.

75
00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.630
<v Speaker 2>When we talk about the plain commands of religion.

76
00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:56.110
<v Speaker 1>The daily laws, the rituals, the physical actions required of a believer.

77
00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:59.950
<v Speaker 2>They can very easily become empty mechanical rituals.

78
00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:03.910
<v Speaker 2>If you only have the outer law, you are essentially just reading the sheet music.

79
00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:07.710
<v Speaker 1>But the inner interpretation is what actually unlocks the symphony.

80
00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:09.750
<v Speaker 1>It is the spiritual meaning

81
00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.790
<v Speaker 1>beneath the physical action.

82
00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:16.110
<v Speaker 2>Remove the guide and the entire structure collapses across all three of those levels.

83
00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:21.230
<v Speaker 1>So the highest truths regarding the unity of God become distorted by human projection.

84
00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:27.070
<v Speaker 2>And the history of the message loses its continuous anchor and fractures into competing narratives.

85
00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:33.310
<v Speaker 2>and the daily law has become hollow, mechanical burdens stripped of their inner meaning.

86
00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:35.630
<v Speaker 1>This is what it means to be a pole and a foundation.

87
00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:39.310
<v Speaker 1>A foundation isn't just one supporting wall among other walls.

88
00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:43.390
<v Speaker 2>No, it is the solitary thing upon which everything else depends to exist at all.

89
00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:50.110
<v Speaker 1>And the author makes it very clear that this architectural framework is not some rogue theory he invented in a vacuum.

90
00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:51.070
<v Speaker 2>Definitely not.

91
00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:57.830
<v Speaker 2>He anchors this structural necessity deeply in the traditions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his.

92
00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.670
<v Speaker 1>That anchor is crucial for his audience.

93
00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:03.870
<v Speaker 1>We have to recall the famous tradition of the two weighty things.

94
00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:09.390
<v Speaker 2>Yes, the prophet is remembered as leaving behind the book of God and his kindred, his living family.

95
00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:11.710
<v Speaker 1>He tied them together in one breath.

96
00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:16.670
<v Speaker 1>declaring that the two shall never part from one another until they return to him.

97
00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:24.590
<v Speaker 2>Meaning the revelation was never meant to be this standalone, static document handed to humanity to decipher on our own.

98
00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:28.190
<v Speaker 2>It arrives permanently lashed to a living family.

99
00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:35.070
<v Speaker 1>Because a book, no matter how divine its origin, cannot answer back when a new century brings a new question.

100
00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:42.630
<v Speaker 2>A text can be misinterpreted, mistranslated, or deliberately manipulated by those in power.

101
00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:50.270
<v Speaker 1>The living guide is the active safeguard of the text, the one who carries its actual intended meaning forward through the friction of time.

102
00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:51.550
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

103
00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:52.270
<v Speaker 2>Which is why.

104
00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:52.830
<v Speaker 1>Wait, hold on.

105
00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:56.190
<v Speaker 1>I have to stop you there because I am feeling some serious friction with this logic.

106
00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:57.630
<v Speaker 1>I don't buy that yet.

107
00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.430
<v Speaker 1>How can the guardian of the revelation outrank the one who brought it?

108
00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:02.190
<v Speaker 2>That's a very fair question.

109
00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:08.030
<v Speaker 1>I mean, inherently, the guy who delivers the law, the founder of the entire system, has to be the CEO, doesn't he?

110
00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:15.630
<v Speaker 1>It seems completely counterintuitive, almost disrespectful to the historical timeline, to say the keeper of the book is more central than the bringer of the book.

111
00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:20.910
<v Speaker 2>That is a highly rational skepticism, and it is exactly the objection the author anticipates.

112
00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:25.630
<v Speaker 2>Addressing it reveals the very core of the classical Ismaili reading.

113
00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:26.990
<v Speaker 1>Okay, so how does he address it?

114
00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:31.630
<v Speaker 2>He meets this head on by surfacing our first central tension.

115
00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:32.830
<v Speaker 2>We can call this tension.

116
00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:35.230
<v Speaker 2>The imam before the messenger.

117
00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:36.830
<v Speaker 1>The imam before the messenger.

118
00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:38.910
<v Speaker 2>He doesn't shy away from the paradox.

119
00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:48.830
<v Speaker 2>He provides 3 brilliant, linked reasons to defend this tension and prove why the continuous office is the larger, more comprehensive affirmation.

120
00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:50.990
<v Speaker 1>Okay, let's break down those three reasons.

121
00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:54.590
<v Speaker 1>How does he justify putting the guardian above the founder?

122
00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:56.750
<v Speaker 2>The first reason is a matter of containment.

123
00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:03.950
<v Speaker 2>The author argues that affirming the imit already automatically affirms the messengership because whoever knows the imam knows the messenger.

124
00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:07.310
<v Speaker 1>Oh, so the larger circle inherently contains the smaller circle?

125
00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:08.110
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

126
00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:15.870
<v Speaker 2>If you acknowledge the living guide, you have in that very act already acknowledged the historical messenger whose law that guide is actively upholding.

127
00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:19.230
<v Speaker 1>Because you cannot guard a revelation that doesn't exist.

128
00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:21.190
<v Speaker 1>Recognizing the guardian proves

129
00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:23.790
<v Speaker 1>you already accept the existence of the revealer.

130
00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.310
<v Speaker 2>But it doesn't work in reverse.

131
00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:32.350
<v Speaker 2>A person can accept that a historical messenger arrived, accept the historical book as a static artifact, and stop right there.

132
00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:36.590
<v Speaker 1>They can completely miss the living guide standing right in front of them in their own era.

133
00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:38.590
<v Speaker 2>That asymmetry is the key.

134
00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:44.830
<v Speaker 2>Proving the imit proves both, but proving only the messengership leaves you short of continuous guidance.

135
00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:49.150
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, the office of the guide is the larger, more encompassing reality.

136
00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:50.350
<v Speaker 1>That makes sense.

137
00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:51.950
<v Speaker 1>What is the second reason?

138
00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:58.750
<v Speaker 2>The second reason he offers is about the audience and understanding which battlefield the intellect actually needs to fight on.

139
00:08:58.790 --> 00:08:59.790
<v Speaker 1>Which battlefield?

140
00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:04.190
<v Speaker 2>Proving the existence of a historical messenger is an argument you make to outsiders.

141
00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:09.390
<v Speaker 2>You make it to polytheists or people who stand entirely outside the religion and have never heard the summons.

142
00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:16.830
<v Speaker 1>But to the people who are already inside the faith, the historical messenger is an established, universally accepted fact.

143
00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:19.550
<v Speaker 1>The real conflict is inside the house.

144
00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:20.390
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

145
00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:24.990
<v Speaker 2>Believers inside the community are not drawing swords over whether the historical messenger existed.

146
00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:30.110
<v Speaker 2>They are arguing, often violently, about who the true guide is right now.

147
00:09:30.590 --> 00:09:38.110
<v Speaker 1>The real intellectual labor, the debate that must be won with evidence and profound reasoning rather than just historical assertion

148
00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:40.750
<v Speaker 1>revolves entirely around the living guide.

149
00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:47.390
<v Speaker 2>You prove the messenger to the outsider, but you must prove the guide to the believers who are already inside but fiercely divided.

150
00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:48.830
<v Speaker 1>That frames it perfectly.

151
00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:54.110
<v Speaker 1>The author is focusing the intellectual energy on the most vital active dispute.

152
00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:56.590
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us to the third reason.

153
00:09:56.990 --> 00:09:59.710
<v Speaker 1>And this one feels like it carries the most ontological weight.

154
00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:01.470
<v Speaker 2>It is the deepest of the three.

155
00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:04.190
<v Speaker 2>The third reason is about the permanence of the office.

156
00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:09.150
<v Speaker 2>The author states that the world can never, not for a fraction of a second, be without a living guide.

157
00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:10.670
<v Speaker 1>But messengers are different, right?

158
00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:14.990
<v Speaker 2>Messengers, however, are present at specific junctures and absent at others.

159
00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:17.790
<v Speaker 2>Prophecy comes in discrete historical cycles.

160
00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:23.150
<v Speaker 1>A prophet speaks, delivers a new law, establishes A framework, and then departs.

161
00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:25.710
<v Speaker 2>While the guide remains continuously,

162
00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:34.750
<v Speaker 2>A guidance that switches on and off, that exists brightly in one century but goes entirely dark in the next, cannot be the true foundation of reality.

163
00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:38.910
<v Speaker 1>Because a foundation has to always be there, bearing the weight of the structure above it.

164
00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:48.070
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, the office that is permanently, continuously present must inherently be more fundamental than the office that only appears sporadically to deliver law.

165
00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:50.990
<v Speaker 1>That is a staggering way to view human history.

166
00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:54.270
<v Speaker 1>I mean, prophecy is almost like a specialized task.

167
00:10:54.830 --> 00:11:01.790
<v Speaker 1>a massive world-altering task, but a specific one that occurs only when a new law is required by a new era.

168
00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:06.750
<v Speaker 2>But the underlying eternal state of the world requires the constant unbroken presence of the guide.

169
00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.630
<v Speaker 1>So how does he prove that prophecy is just a phase?

170
00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:18.270
<v Speaker 2>To prove that prophecy is a phase while guidance is the eternal state, the author asks us to look at how these massive titles trade places within scripture itself.

171
00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:20.830
<v Speaker 2>He points out a fascinating textual detail.

172
00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:21.470
<v Speaker 1>What detail?

173
00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:26.030
<v Speaker 2>God called Ishmael a messenger as recorded in chapter 19, verse 54.

174
00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:28.350
<v Speaker 1>So Ishmael holds the specific title of messenger.

175
00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:30.110
<v Speaker 2>But contrast that with Abraham.

176
00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:38.830
<v Speaker 2>Look at the famous address to Abraham in chapter 2, verse 124, where God says, indeed I am appointing you an imam for all humanity.

177
00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:48.550
<v Speaker 1>The author is very careful to point out that this supreme title was bestowed upon Abraham only at the very end of his life, long after he had already completed all his arduous work as a messenger.

178
00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:51.870
<v Speaker 2>That completely shifts the traditional Sunday school timeline.

179
00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:57.230
<v Speaker 2>We tend to think of these titles as synonymous, or we assume a prophet is the highest possible rank.

180
00:11:57.310 --> 00:12:00.830
<v Speaker 1>But this implies the title of Imam was not Abraham's starting point.

181
00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:02.750
<v Speaker 1>It was his ultimate destination.

182
00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:07.950
<v Speaker 1>It was the crown he earned after passing a lifetime of severe, crushing trials.

183
00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:15.950
<v Speaker 2>To reinforce how monumental the shift is, the author cites A profound tradition from the 6th Imam, which outlines this exact ladder of ascent.

184
00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:17.150
<v Speaker 1>How does that ladder work?

185
00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:18.510
<v Speaker 2>It details how God

186
00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:31.070
<v Speaker 2>as a servant, then elevated him to a prophet, then elevated him to a friend, then elevated him to a messenger, and only at the very end, at the absolute summit of this grueling progression, declared him an imam for all humanity.

187
00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:33.870
<v Speaker 1>That makes the mechanics of the hierarchy undeniable.

188
00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:37.550
<v Speaker 1>Messengership is a crucial step on the ladder.

189
00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:39.950
<v Speaker 1>But it's not the top rung.

190
00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:45.790
<v Speaker 2>Prophecy is a wave that rises up out of the ocean when the historical moment requires a new law to be spoken.

191
00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:49.150
<v Speaker 2>It crashes onto the shore of history, reshaping the landscape.

192
00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:56.910
<v Speaker 1>But guidance, the emmett, is the deep, silent, abiding ocean that the wave ultimately returns to.

193
00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:58.430
<v Speaker 1>The wave subsides,

194
00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:00.030
<v Speaker 1>but the ocean remains.

195
00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:06.510
<v Speaker 2>This unbroken continuity is exactly what the first imam, peace be upon him, taught his closest confidants.

196
00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:10.350
<v Speaker 1>The father of imams outlined this eternal presence beautifully, didn't he?

197
00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:15.710
<v Speaker 2>Taught that the earth is never, for a single fraction of a moment, left without one who stands for God with a proof.

